#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:41
#, no-c-format
msgid "&kolf; is a miniature golf game for &kde;."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: keyword
#: index.docbook:48
#, no-c-format
msgid "<keyword>KDE</keyword>"
msgstr "<keyword>KDE</keyword>"

#. Tag: keyword
#: index.docbook:49
#, no-c-format
msgid "golf"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: keyword
#: index.docbook:50
#, no-c-format
msgid "miniature"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: keyword
#: index.docbook:51
#, no-c-format
msgid "arcade"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:56
#, no-c-format
msgid "Introduction"
msgstr "Introduccion"

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:57
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&kolf; is a miniature golf game with block graphics and a 2d top-down view. "
"Courses are dynamic, and up to 10 people can play at once in competition."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:58
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&kolf; comes with a tutorial course. <link linkend=\"tutorial\">Playing this "
"course</link> is the easiest way to get started."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:63
#, no-c-format
msgid "Tutorial"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:65
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&kolf;'s game-play is simple. It is easiest to learn how to play hands-on, "
"and thus a tutorial course is included to guide you through the basics. This "
"chapter will guide you through this tutorial. To start the tutorial, choose "
"<menuchoice><guimenu>Help</guimenu><guimenuitem>Tutorial</guimenuitem></"
"menuchoice>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:68
#, no-c-format
msgid "Hitting the Ball"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:69
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"To aim the ball, either line up the mouse behind the ball so the putter aims "
"in the direction you want to hit it or press one of the <keycap>Left Arrow</"
"keycap> (counterclockwise) or <keycap>Right Arrow</keycap> (clockwise) keys, "
"to rotate the putter."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:73
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"To rotate the putter more quickly with the keyboard, hold down "
"<keycap>Shift</keycap> while pressing or holding the arrow keys. To rotate "
"more slowly, hold down <keycap>Control</keycap>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:75
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"To hit the ball, press and hold either the &LMB; or <keycap>Down Arrow</"
"keycap> key. The longer you hold down the key or button, the more strength "
"the putt will have. It will take practice to get a good feel for speed."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:80
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"To stop abort the putt while you are still holding moving the putter back in "
"your stroke, press <keycap>Escape</keycap>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:83
#, no-c-format
msgid "Hit the ball into the hole to move on to the next."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:87 index.docbook:298
#, no-c-format
msgid "Slopes"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:88
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Slopes are slanted areas of the course that the golf ball rolls down in an "
"expected manner. The light of the golf course is from the upper-left, so the "
"brightest slopes will push the ball towards the upper-left corner of the "
"hole. Choose <menuchoice><guimenu>Hole</guimenu><guimenuitem>Show Info</"
"guimenuitem></menuchoice> to see an arrow that points in the direction the "
"slope will roll the ball."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:89
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"On this tutorial hole, hit the ball onto the upward-pointing slope and let "
"it roll into the cup to move onto the next hole."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:92
#, no-c-format
msgid "Directions"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:93
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"On the next hole, experiment with the other kinds of slopes. There are "
"elliptical, vertical, horizontal, and diagonal slopes in &kolf;. Hit the "
"ball into the hole."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:95
#, no-c-format
msgid "Steepness"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:96
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The next tutorial hole has two slopes of differing steepness. If you choose "
"<menuchoice><guimenu>Hole</guimenu><guimenuitem>Show Info</guimenuitem></"
"menuchoice>, the steepness of the slope will appear as a number from 1-8, "
"where 8 is steepest and 1 is shallowest. Hit the ball into the hole and "
"continue to the next hole."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:100
#, no-c-format
msgid "Walls"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:101
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Balls bounce of walls as one would expect them to, with a little bit of "
"dampening. Deflect the ball off the walls and into the cup to advance to the "
"next hole."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:104 index.docbook:327
#, no-c-format
msgid "Puddles and Sand"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:105
#, no-c-format
msgid "Puddles"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:106
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Puddles are blue. When your ball rolls into a puddle, a penalty stroke is "
"added to your score and you must then choose where you want to take your "
"next shot from: either next to the puddle or from the same place as where "
"you just took the last shot."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:108
#, no-c-format
msgid "Sand"
msgstr "Sable"

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:109
#, no-c-format
msgid "Sand is yellow. Balls roll through sand very slowly."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:110
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Hit the ball around the puddles and through the sand into the hole to "
"continue to the next tutorial hole."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:114 index.docbook:348
#, no-c-format
msgid "Windmills"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:115
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Windmills are compound objects with brown border walls and a moving arm on "
"one side. Half-walls create the opening to the windmill, which the black arm "
"swings across. It takes practice to be able to time the stroke of the ball "
"to pass through the opening. Hit the ball through the windmill into the hole "
"to continue."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:118
#, no-c-format
msgid "Black Holes"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:119
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"When you hit your ball into a black hole, it is transported to the exit and "
"ejected at the angle of the exit at a speed directly relational to the speed "
"your ball was going. Choose <menuchoice><guimenu>Hole</"
"guimenu><guimenuitem>Show Info</guimenuitem></menuchoice> to see which Black "
"Hole goes to which exit and which direction the ball will come out of the "
"exit. The rim around Black Holes are the same color as their corresponding "
"exits. Hit the ball into the black hole, which will then eject the ball into "
"the cup so you can go to the next hole."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:122 index.docbook:363
#, no-c-format
msgid "Floaters"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:123
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Floaters are moving platforms that carry a ball that lands on it. Floaters' "
"speeds vary. This one is a pretty slow floater, but you still must time your "
"shot so you can go over the wall and into the cup."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:126
#, no-c-format
msgid "Bridges"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:127
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Bridges are simple - they just lift the ball above the things below them, "
"like slopes, sand, and puddles. They can have brown walls on their perimeter."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:131
#, no-c-format
msgid "Basics"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:132
#, no-c-format
msgid "Here's in-depth description of &kolf;'s basic features."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:135
#, no-c-format
msgid "Starting a Game"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:137
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"To start a game, choose <menuchoice><guimenu>Game</guimenu><guimenuitem>New</"
"guimenuitem></menuchoice>. This opens a dialog where you can configure your "
"new game."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:142
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"More third-party &kolf; courses are available at the &kolf; website. See "
"<ulink url=\"http://web.mit.edu/jasonkb/www/kolf/\">the &kolf; user-uploaded "
"courses page</ulink>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:147
#, no-c-format
msgid "<guilabel>Players</guilabel> Page"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:148
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"To add a player to your game, click the <guibutton>New Player</guibutton> "
"button."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:151
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The text boxes in the grass area are the names of the players in this game. "
"To change the name of the players, change the text in the text boxes. To "
"change the ball color of the players' balls, click the middle button that "
"has a color rectangle, and a dialog will open where you can select the new "
"ball color. The rectangle on the button is the current player's ball color."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:158
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"To remove a player, click the <guibutton>Remove</guibutton> button next to "
"the player's name."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:163
#, no-c-format
msgid "<guilabel>Course</guilabel> Page"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:164
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"To choose the course that you want to play, click its entry in the list box "
"on the left of this page. &kolf; comes with many built-in courses that you "
"can play."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:165
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"To add a course that you have downloaded or made, click on the "
"<guibutton>Add...</guibutton> button on the bottom of this page, and choose "
"the course file in the file dialog."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:167
#, no-c-format
msgid "<guilabel>Options</guilabel> Page"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:168
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"On this page you can choose whether or not this game will be played in "
"<guilabel>Strict mode</guilabel>. In strict mode, there is no undo, moving "
"holes, or editing of the course. This mode is generally for competition. "
"Only in strict mode are high scores kept."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:172
#, no-c-format
msgid "Saved Games"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:173
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&kolf; can keep a history of your rounds that are still in progress and save "
"them to disk so you can load them and play them later. Saved games include "
"the current hole, names and colors of all players in game, and the scores of "
"the players."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:174
#, no-c-format
msgid "Saving Your Game"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:175
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"To save your game while still playing, choose <menuchoice><guimenu>Game</"
"guimenu><guimenuitem>Save Game</guimenuitem> </menuchoice>. If you have not "
"saved a game on this game before, you will be prompted for the file to save "
"to."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:178
#, no-c-format
msgid "Loading Your Game"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:179
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"To load the game that you saved, choose <menuchoice><guimenu>Game</"
"guimenu><guimenuitem>Load Saved Game...</guimenuitem> </menuchoice>. You can "
"then choose the saved game to open."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:184
#, no-c-format
msgid "Practicing"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:185
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"There are a few features that make practicing your golf easier. These "
"features are not enabled during strict mode."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:186
#, no-c-format
msgid "Undo"
msgstr "Anullar"

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:187
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"To undo a bad shot, choose <menuchoice><guimenu>Hole</"
"guimenu><guimenuitem>Undo Shot</guimenuitem></menuchoice>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:189
#, no-c-format
msgid "Switching Holes"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:190
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The <guimenu>Go</guimenu> menu contains many menu items for switching to "
"different holes that are quite self explanatory. See the <link linkend=\"go-"
"menu\"><guimenu>Go</guimenu> menu reference</link>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:194
#, no-c-format
msgid "Putting Options"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:195
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&kolf; has a few ways to putt. The basic putting method is <link linkend="
"\"hitting\">described above</link>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:196
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The mouse is enabled by default. This means that whenever the mouse is "
"moved, the putter aligns to the line between the mouse and ball."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:197
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If you prefer to only use the keyboard and arrow keys, you can disable mouse "
"putting by unchecking <menuchoice><guimenu>Settings</"
"guimenu><guimenuitem>Enable Mouse for Moving Putter</guimenuitem></"
"menuchoice>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:199
#, no-c-format
msgid "Advanced Putting"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:200
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Advanced putting can be enabled by checking <menuchoice><guimenu>Settings</"
"guimenu><guimenuitem>Enable Advanced Putting</guimenuitem></menuchoice>. "
"This putting mode is slightly more challenging than the regular click-and-"
"hold mode, and can be used with either the mouse or keyboard."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:202
#, no-c-format
msgid "To putt in this mode:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:205
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Click or press the <keycap>Down Arrow</keycap> key once to begin the swing. "
"The putting indicator will appear, and the strength gauge will start filling "
"up with a color, which changes as the gauge fills up."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:208
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Click or press the <keycap>Down Arrow</keycap> key when the strength gauge "
"has filled up the amount that you want. The farther around the circle the "
"strength gauge has filled up, the stronger the putt will be. It takes "
"practice to be able to get the correct strength on putts."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:209
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"After this, the color in the putting indicator will start to wind down back "
"to where it started."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:212
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Click or press the <keycap>Down Arrow</keycap> key again when the strength-"
"indicator is as close as possible to the center line of the putting "
"indicator."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:215
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The closer the final click (or key press) is, the more true the putt is to "
"the original line."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:220
#, no-c-format
msgid "Editing Courses"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:221
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&kolf; comes with a versatile editor for its course file format. Courses can "
"have any number of holes, and there is no limiting the creativity of the "
"course designer."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:223
#, no-c-format
msgid "Creating a New Course"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:224
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"To create a new course, choose <menuchoice><guimenu>Game</"
"guimenu><guimenuitem>New</guimenuitem></menuchoice>. Go to the "
"<guilabel>Course</guilabel> page of the dialog, and choose the "
"<guilabel>Create New</guilabel> course. Make sure that, under the "
"<guilabel>Options</guilabel> page of the dialog, <guilabel>Strict mode</"
"guilabel> is unchecked."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:227
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"To play this new course later, click the <guibutton>Add...</guibutton> "
"button to add the course to your list of courses."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:231
#, no-c-format
msgid "Adding Holes"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:232
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Choose <menuchoice><guimenu>Hole</guimenu><guimenuitem>New</guimenuitem></"
"menuchoice> to add a new hole at the end of the course."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:234
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You can restore a hole to a blank state, similar to when it was first "
"created, by choosing <menuchoice><guimenu>Hole</guimenu><guimenuitem>Clear</"
"guimenuitem></menuchoice>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:236
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Two items will appear on new holes: a cup, and a white ball. The white ball "
"marks where players' balls start the hole."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:239
#, no-c-format
msgid "Editing Basics"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:240
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The basic building block of &kolf; courses is the <quote>object</quote>. "
"Holes are made up of a bunch of objects. Objects can be moved to any "
"location, and the different kinds of objects each have different properties "
"you can set."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:241
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"To start editing a hole, toggle the <menuchoice><guimenu>Hole</"
"guimenu><guimenuitem>Edit</guimenuitem></menuchoice> menu item, or press the "
"pencil icon on the toolbar. To leave editing mode, uncheck this menu item "
"(or the toolbar icon)."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:243
#, no-c-format
msgid "Adding Objects"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:244
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"To add a new object to the course, choose its name in the list box labeled "
"<guilabel>Add object:</guilabel>. The object will appear in the center of "
"the course."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:246
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Bridges and signs will cover up new objects if they were already in the "
"center of the hole."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:249
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You can add more than one cup to a hole! Doing so can create interesting "
"hole designs."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:251
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Some &kolf; objects are available as plugins. If you add these objects to "
"your course, course players will need to download the plugin."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:252
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Third-party &kolf; plugins are available at the &kolf; website. See <ulink "
"url=\"http://web.mit.edu/jasonkb/www/kolf/\">the &kolf; plugins download "
"page</ulink>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:255
#, no-c-format
msgid "Moving and Resizing Objects"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:256
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Move your mouse cursor over the object that you want to move, and the mouse "
"cursor will change to a hand cursor. Then click and drag the object to where "
"you want it."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:257
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Puddles, sand, floaters, bridges, and signs can be resized by dragging the "
"small circle at their lower-right corner."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:260
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Walls can be moved by clicking and dragging their endpoints, or you can move "
"the whole wall by clicking and dragging on the middle part of the wall as "
"normal."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:264
#, no-c-format
msgid "Objects' Settings"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:265
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Objects' properties can be changed in the area at the lower-right corner of "
"the &kolf; window. To edit an object, move your mouse cursor over it until "
"the cursor changes to the hand cursor. Then click. Various controls for that "
"object will appear in the lower-right corner of the &kolf; window."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:266
#, no-c-format
msgid "General Hole Settings"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:267
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"To edit general hole settings, click an area of the hole that is not covered "
"by an object. When this is possible, the cursor will be the normal pointer "
"cursor."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guilabel
#: index.docbook:270
#, no-c-format
msgid "Course name:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:272
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This is the name that is shown in the new game dialog and in the "
"<guimenuitem>About Course</guimenuitem> dialog."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guilabel
#: index.docbook:275
#, no-c-format
msgid "Course author:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:277
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This is the author name that is shown in the <guimenuitem>About Course</"
"guimenuitem> dialog."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guilabel
#: index.docbook:280
#, no-c-format
msgid "Par:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:282
#, no-c-format
msgid "The par of the hole."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guilabel
#: index.docbook:285
#, no-c-format
msgid "Maximum:"
msgstr "Maximum :"

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:287
#, no-c-format
msgid "The maximum number of strokes a player can take on the hole."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guilabel
#: index.docbook:290
#, no-c-format
msgid "Show border walls"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:292
#, no-c-format
msgid "Whether or not to show walls around the border of the hole."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: term
#: index.docbook:301
#, no-c-format
msgid "Type"
msgstr "Tipe"

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:303
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Choose the type of the slope - <guilabel>Vertical</guilabel>, "
"<guilabel>Horizontal</guilabel>, <guilabel>Diagonal</guilabel>, "
"<guilabel>Opposite Diagonal</guilabel>, <guilabel>Circular</guilabel>. "
"Diagonal and opposite diagonal slopes are triangles, while circular slopes "
"are circles. Vertical and horizontal slopes are rectangles of course."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guilabel
#: index.docbook:306
#, no-c-format
msgid "Reverse direction"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:308
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Whether or not to reverse the slant of the slope. For example, consider an "
"elliptical slope (a circular slope). When it is not reversed, it pushes "
"balls outward like a mound. When it is reversed, it sucks balls inward like "
"a hole."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guilabel
#: index.docbook:311
#, no-c-format
msgid "Grade:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:313
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Choose the grade (steepness) of the slope by moving the slider so the grade "
"moves towards 0 or 8, where 8 is steepest and 0 is flat. Steeper slopes push "
"the ball more."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guilabel
#: index.docbook:316
#, no-c-format
msgid "Unmovable"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:318
#, no-c-format
msgid "Whether or not this can be moved by other objects, like floaters."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:320
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If a floater overlaps with this object at any point on this path, and the "
"slope is large and thus won't move the floater, you <emphasis>must</"
"emphasis> make the slope <guilabel>Unmovable</guilabel>, or &kolf; will "
"suffer poor performance!"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:328
#, no-c-format
msgid "Puddles and Sand have the same configuration options as each other."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guilabel
#: index.docbook:331
#, no-c-format
msgid "Enable show/hide"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:333
#, no-c-format
msgid "Checking this enables the flashing of this puddle or sand."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: term
#: index.docbook:336
#, no-c-format
msgid "Show/Hide speed"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:338
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The more towards <guilabel>Fast</guilabel> the slider is, the faster the "
"puddle or sand flashes."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:344
#, no-c-format
msgid "Bridges, Windmills, Floaters, and Signs"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:345
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Bridges, windmills, floaters, and signs all have similar settings for "
"configuring on which sides there are border walls. To show the wall on a "
"side, check the check box for that side."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:346
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"There are also some more specific options for windmills, floaters, and signs."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guilabel
#: index.docbook:350
#, no-c-format
msgid "Windmill on bottom"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:352
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Whether or not the black windmill arm is on bottom or top - the default is "
"that it is on the bottom. The two half-walls will always be with the arm. "
"Note that you cannot enable a border wall on the side that the arm is."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: term
#: index.docbook:355
#, no-c-format
msgid "Speed"
msgstr "Velocitat"

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:357
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The more towards <guilabel>Fast</guilabel> the slider is, the faster the "
"black windmill arm moves."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:364
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The path the floater moves along is shown by the wall it's connected to."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guilabel
#: index.docbook:366
#, no-c-format
msgid "Moving speed"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:368
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The more towards <guilabel>Fast</guilabel> the slider is, the faster the "
"floater moves. If the slider is all the way at the <guilabel>Slow</guilabel> "
"end, the floater will stop."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:374
#, no-c-format
msgid "Signs"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: guilabel
#: index.docbook:376
#, no-c-format
msgid "Sign HTML:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:378
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You can enter any valid &HTML; here. For example, entering <markup>Hit it "
"&lt;i&gt;softly&lt;/i&gt; towards the slope!</markup> will create the text "
"<quote>Hit it <emphasis>softly</emphasis> towards the slope!</quote>."
msgstr ""

